// wallet and stayed in /learn/bitcoin-keys §6.
---
<Base
  title="The self-custody configuration ladder · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Every serious Bitcoin self-custody setup fits on one ladder of rising complexity — from single-signature to collaborative multisig. Find the lowest rung that covers you, and stop there."
  verified="2026-07-15"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">102 · Your setup · lesson 1 of 2</p>
    <h1>The configuration <span class="accent">ladder</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">The major self-custody setups arrange themselves into a ladder of rising complexity. Each rung solves a real problem — and introduces new ways to fail.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">How to use it</span>
      <p>Don’t ask “what’s the most secure setup?” Ask “<strong>what’s the simplest setup that adequately covers the risks I can actually name?</strong>” Find that rung, build it, test it — and don’t climb higher until a real reason pushes you up. Complexity you don’t fully control is itself a threat.</p>
    </div>
    <RuleBand />
    <p class="sub">New to keys, seed words, and what “signing” means? <a href="/learn/bitcoin-keys">Go back to what a key actually is →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="config-head"><span class="config-eyebrow">Wallet configuration</span><span class="config-sub">how your keys are arranged — the ladder proper</span></p>
    <Ladder />
  </section>

  <section class="prose">

    <h2>Before the rungs: how much belongs at each temperature</h2>
    <p>
      The last lesson covered what <strong>hot</strong> and <strong>cold</strong> mean. This is the part
      that’s actually a decision: how much of your money sits at each temperature. Experienced holders
      rarely use one wallet for everything — they run a few, at different temperatures, and let money flow
      between them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A hot wallet</strong> holds a small amount for spending. If your phone is lost or
        compromised this balance could go with it — but because it’s small, that’s survivable. If you use
        Lightning for everyday payments, those funds have to be hot by design.</li>
      <li><strong>A cold wallet</strong> — a hardware wallet — holds the balance you’re not spending this
        week but still want within reach. For most people this single device is the core of the whole setup.</li>
      <li><strong>Deep cold storage</strong> holds the bulk, touched rarely. This is where the higher rungs
        of the ladder below earn their keep, and it only becomes worth the extra effort once you’re holding
        a substantial amount.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Money flows one way as your balance grows: income lands hot, gets swept to cold, and — once cold holds
      more than you need soon — moves to deep cold. Spending reverses it: big needs come from the coldest
      tier, everyday spending from the hot one. The payoff is that you’re never <em>afraid to touch</em> your
      savings, because you haven’t mixed them with your spending money.
    </p>
    <p>
      Match the effort to the amount. Starting small? A phone wallet is fine — the best wallet is the one
      you’ll actually use and understand. Past pocket money, add a hardware wallet and keep only spending
      money on the phone. You may also hear people insist on strict <strong>air-gapping</strong> — a device
      that never plugs in and communicates only by scanned QR codes. It’s the most isolated form of cold
      storage, but for most people a well-managed hardware wallet that plugs in by USB is already strong
      enough. The discipline you bring matters more than the air gap.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the ladder</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Most newcomers</strong> belong on rung 1 (single-signature) and should stay there through their first year.</li>
      <li><strong>Most substantial holders</strong> settle at rung 3 or 4 (self-run or collaborative multi-signature).</li>
      <li><strong>Bigger multi-signature (3-of-5)</strong> is right for a small minority — large holdings or genuine multi-party needs. It’s the same technology as 2-of-3, so it lives on the <a href="/learn/ladder/multisig">multi-signature rung</a> rather than as a separate step.</li>
      <li>Jumping straight to the top introduces complexity you’re not yet equipped to manage. That complexity is a bigger risk than the ones it’s meant to solve.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Optional · Advanced backup</span>
      <p><strong>Shamir backup</strong> (SLIP-39) is a way to split a single seed’s backup into shares — any few rebuild it, no one share reveals anything. It’s a <em>backup method</em>, not a signing configuration, so it sits beside the ladder rather than on it. <a href="/learn/ladder/shamir">How Shamir backup works →</a></p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="label">{bip85.name}</span>
      <p set:html={bip85.note.replace('BIP-85', '<strong>BIP-85</strong>')}></p>
    </div>

  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Which rung is yours?</span>
      <p>This page teaches the ladder; it doesn’t place you on it. The <a href="/quiz">2-minute quiz</a> does that — six plain questions, and it names the rung that fits what you’re actually protecting against. It errs toward the <em>lowest</em> rung that covers you, because that’s the rule this whole ladder is built on.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/quiz" class="btn btn-primary">Find my rung <span class="arw">→</span></a>
      <a href="/learn" class="btn btn-ghost">Learn the hands-on skills first</a>
    </div>
  </section>


  <LessonNav />
</Base>


